From: Sobs <antohasobne@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] video: bareapp: OpenGL pictures copied by the GPU, read a
 frame later

SDL_BAREAPP_GL_ASYNC=1 (a game's game.ini [env]): at each swap the GPU
copies the picture into one of two renderbuffers, scaled to the size it
gets in bare_app's frame, with a fence; the copy made at the swap before
(finished by then) is read back and shown. The game's work on the next
frame overlaps the GPU's on this one instead of waiting for it in
glReadPixels, and a scaled picture costs no CPU; the picture is a frame
late. When the next swap is late in coming (a game loading, a picture
drawn once that waits for a key), the picture is shown from the game's
event loop 50 ms on, once the GPU has copied it (no waiting); a game that
pumps no events meanwhile gets frames more than 100 ms apart shown at
their own swap. Mesa's panfrost reads pixel buffer objects back on the
CPU too, so those would not help. Desktop OpenGL reads GL_BGRA, bare_app's
own layout: a plain copy. Each context is looked at when it first swaps:
it needs OpenGL (ES) 3 by its version string (eglGetProcAddress has
something for every name) and a window without multisampling (a scaling
blit cannot resolve it); else, and by default, pictures are read at each
swap.

Both ways the window is read whatever the game has bound and set: draw
and read framebuffers apart, a pixel pack buffer and the pack row length
and skips (OpenGL (ES) 3; desktop OpenGL) too. The copies belong to the
context they were made in: when it is deleted (SDL has made it no longer
current) or another one swaps, they are forgotten, not deleted in a
context they are not part of.
